dition to there  being variability in terms of the levels  of functioning and profiles of  functioning in the individual with ASD  there's also very various kinds of  measures to assess cognitive functioning  these are some of the common test  batteries we have nonverbal measures and  developmental assessments all of these  test batteries so even taking the top  group all of these measure overall  intellectual or overall cognitive  ability but what's interesting is that  they may measure it in very different  ways so what we find is not only are  there discrepancies discrepant abilities  in the child with ASD but there are also  differences in the way tests measure  cognitive ability and they can differ  along these lines the level of language  skills that are required of that person  taking the test the degree of complexity  of the instructions  level of structure that's provided for  example how much teaching is allowed on  the test items the the extent of the  social demand so how much interaction  there is when the person has to complete  the item whether or not time tasks are  used and how much motor involvement  there is so it becomes incumbent upon  the person who's selecting and  administering the test as well as  consumers of the information to have an  understanding of how these tests differ  because you can imagine that given the  discrepancy and abilities and then given  the various ways in which cognition can  be measured you can optimize or diminish  a person's performance based on the the  kind of measure that you select the  other thing I want to mention in this  context in addition to thinking  carefully about the scores that are  yielded by these measures we also want  to be careful about integrating our  observations with the test results so  the numbers are important but they're  not the end of the story in some ways  they're only the beginning and our  observations can be as important if not  more important in some cases we want to  integrate ou