age disorder  and so on so let me just touch upon that  briefly among the pervasive  developmental disorders we have some  very specific criteria for how diagnoses  are made and these include the number  and combination of symptoms and then  also particular onset criteria that  distinguished autism Asperger's  Asperger's disorder and pdd-nos  so we're looking at both the number and  severity of symptoms and we're also  looking at on set in order to make that  distinction from a classification point  of view what's interesting is that in  the literature there's also some  clinical features that seem to  differentiate especially in the higher  functioning individuals high-functioning  individuals with autism and Asperger's  syndrome  now this is still an area that is is is  being discussed in the literature  whether or not these are two distinct  groups of individuals but thus far there  are certain features that have been  identified that seemed to be more  characteristic of individuals with  high-functioning autism and asperger  disorder and just to give you some  examples in each of those domains so for  example one feature clinical feature  that seems to present more frequently in  high-functioning autism is in terms of  social interaction is greater social  isolation a more aloof presentation or a  more passive presentation when  interacting with others whereas an  Asperger's syndrome often there's  greater social approach and although and  the person appears very interested in  others although the initiations might be  very awkward from a communication  standpoint just highlighting a couple of  things in autism we might see that more  characteristic monotone speech pattern  that dr. volkmar demonstrated or we  might see a history of absent or delayed  language an Asperger's disorder by  definition early language skills are  preserved and what we tend to see is  more verbosity where the person is  talking at you almost in a professorial  pedantic way another feature in the  behaviors category that distinct  clinical feature that sometimes can  distinguish individuals the  high-functioning autism Asperger's  syndrome is a type of interest so in  autism we might see some some narrow  interests or some splinter skills so  some very isolated ability area such as  somebody who has excellent drawing  skills for example and can reproduce a  drawing in extreme and perfect detail an  Asperger's disorder what we tend to see  is some narrow circumscribed interests  that are all absorbing and that tend to  be the amassing of information about  that particular topic  and I'm kind of a debt kind of a  dedication to thinking about and  acquiring information on that subject  area  all right the other area so that one  area of differential diagnosis is within  the PD D spectrum that other area that  were sometimes faced with in terms of  differential diagnosis is thinking about  other disorders that might be presenting  rather than an autism spectrum disorder  and these include again intellectual  disability language disorders  obsessive-compulsive disorder  schizophrenia and so on and some of that  you know for each of these that how we  make the distinction is more complex  than what I'm about to say but some here  are some of the things that we might  look at so for example in an  intellectual disability we might expect  to see a pattern of more even  development so where the person's  language their social functioning their  overall cognitive ability is more even  it might all be delayed relative to the  person's cognitive well relative to the  person's chronological age but we  wouldn't see necessarily those marked  discrepancies and that we do see in  pervasive developmental disorders in  language disorders one of the things  that we might see that distinguishes a  child from a child with autism spectrum  disorder is the use of nonverbal  communication to compensate for the  language impairment so whereas in  individuals with ASD both